course: dictionary
lesson: 4278
title: Greece: a country and an ancient world
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Greece is a proper noun, the name of one special country, so it always starts with a capital letter. When you meet a name like this in a text, read the words around it, because they are its context and often give clues. «Socrates lived about 2,400 years ago in Athens, in Greece.» Athens is a city, so Greece must be the country that Athens is in.
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. «Greece is full of mountains, sea and islands.» Here Greece means the country today, a place on the map. But «The wise owl idea comes from ancient Greece» means the old world of the ancient Greeks, who lived long ago. Ask yourself: is the text talking about a place now, or about the past?
explain: Use the clues to check your meaning. «Instead of one country, the Greeks lived in hundreds of separate city-states, each called a polis.» The words instead of one country tell you the ancient Greeks were not one big country like today. A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: Greece is a country in south-east Europe, by the Mediterranean Sea, with many islands.
fact: The capital city of Greece is Athens.
fact: Ancient Greece was the world of the ancient Greeks, famous for the Olympics, myths, democracy and great thinkers.
fact: «Greece» is a proper noun, so it always starts with a capital letter.
fact: A polis was a city-state, with its own government, army and laws.
exercise: What is Greece? | *A country in south-east Europe, by the Mediterranean Sea | A large city in Italy | A type of ancient coin | A river in Africa
exercise: In «Socrates lived about 2,400 years ago in Athens, in Greece.», what is Athens? | An island in the Atlantic | A goddess of wisdom | *The capital city of Greece | A country
exercise: Which things were the ancient Greeks famous for? | Snow and skiing | Trains, cars and aeroplanes | Pyramids and mummies | *The Olympics, myths, democracy and great thinkers
test: Instead of one country, what did the Greeks live in? | *Hundreds of separate city-states, each called a polis | One single village | One huge empire | Floating ships on the sea
test: In the owl idea, the owl was the bird of Athena. What was Athena the goddess of? | Music and dance | Fire and war | *Wisdom | Sea and storms
exam: Why does the word «Greece» start with a capital letter? | *Because it is a proper noun, the name of a country | Because it is a very important word | Because it is the first word of a sentence | Because it is in a title
